<body>

    <header>
        <h1>The Sunk Cost Fallacy</h1>
        <p>Why we continue bad investments, broken projects, and bad habits—and how to stop doing it.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="container">
        
        <!-- What is it section -->
        <section>
            <h2>What is the Sunk Cost Fallacy?</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Sunk Cost Fallacy</strong> is a cognitive bias where a person continues a behavior, endeavor, or investment as a result of previously invested resources (time, money, or effort).</p>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <p><strong>The Core Mistake:</strong> Making decisions based on what you've already lost or spent, rather than looking strictly at future costs and potential rewards.</p>
            </div>
            <p>Because we deeply dislike the feeling of losing or wasting things (Loss Aversion), we throw good money after bad, or waste more time on dead ends, hoping to justify our past choices.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Examples section -->
        <section>
            <h2>Real-World Examples</h2>
            <div class="examples-grid">
                
                <div class="example-card">
                    <h3>1. The Bad Movie Ticket</h3>
                    <p>You buy a $20 movie ticket. Twenty minutes in, you realize the movie is terrible and completely boring. The fallacy tells you to stay until the end so your $20 "isn't wasted." The rational choice? Walk out, because the $20 is gone either way, but you can save your remaining time.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="example-card">
                    <h3>2. Failing Business Ventures</h3>
                    <p>A company pours millions of dollars into a software project that is outdated and failing. Instead of canceling it, leadership invests millions more because "we've already spent so much and cannot quit now."</p>
                </div>

                <div class="example-card">
                    <h3>3. Toxic Relationships</h3>
                    <p>Staying in an unhappy or unhealthy relationship for years solely because of "all the years we've already invested together," ignoring whether the future holds any happiness.</p>
                </div>

            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- How to avoid section -->
        <section>
            <h2>How to Avoid the Fallacy</h2>
            <p>Overcoming this mental trap requires shifting your focus from the past to the future. Here are practical ways to beat it:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Ignore Past Investments:</strong> When making a choice, pretend you are starting fresh today with zero history attached to the situation. Ask yourself: <em>"Would I start this project/relationship right now if I hadn't invested anything before?"</em></li>
                <li><strong>Define Exit Strategies Early:</strong> Before entering a major project or investment, set clear objective boundaries (e.g., maximum budget or timeline) where you will re-evaluate or walk away.</li>
                <li><strong>Seek an Outside Perspective:</strong> Talk to a trusted friend, mentor, or colleague who has no emotional attachment to what you've already spent. They will see the situation objectively.</li>
                <li><strong>Accept Losses as Tuition:</strong> Reframe past losses not as "waste," but as tuition paid for a valuable life or business lesson.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

    </div>

